rules.requirement:
A landlord or agent may not threaten or take reprisals against a residential tenant for:
- enforcing housing laws through judicial or administrative action;
- reporting code violations to the board of health or another authority, or in writing to the landlord;
- organizing or joining a tenants' union;
- certain other protected activities.

Damages are the greater of 1 to 3 months' rent or actual damages, plus costs and attorney's fees. A rebuttable presumption of reprisal arises if, within six months of protected activity, the tenant receives a termination notice (other than for nonpayment), a rent increase or a substantial change in terms. The presumption is rebuttable only by clear and convincing evidence. Lease waivers are void.

coverage_conditions.summary:
Rent ceilings and the AGA apply only to fully covered units:
- most units in multifamily properties built before 1980;
- single-family homes with current tenancies that began before 1996;
- rooming houses (single-family homes with five or more rooms rented individually).

Partially covered units are registered but not rent controlled:
- new construction with a certificate of occupancy after June 1980;
- single-family tenancies from Jan 1, 1996;
- most condominiums;
- certain subsidized units.

Exempt unit types are outside the ordinance.

rules.exemptions:
Partially covered (no rent control):
- new construction with a certificate of occupancy after June 1980;
- single-family tenancies beginning on or after Jan 1, 1996;
- most condominiums;
- Section 202 and Section 811 units;
- Rent Supplement, Section 8 LMSA and Project-based Section 8 units in HUD-insured or HUD-held mortgage projects.

Exempt:
- Golden Duplex;
- owner sharing a kitchen or bath with the tenant (if the owner lived there first);
- ADU-property tenancies after Nov 7, 2018 with an owner-occupied unit;
- university rental units;
- nonprofit cooperatives.

rules.requirement:
Rental housing providers may not ask about or use criminal history or criminal background checks in advertising, applications, tenant selection or decisions.

As to current or prospective tenants, it is unlawful to:
- inquire about criminal history;
- indicate that people with criminal backgrounds will not be considered;
- refuse to rent, or terminate a tenancy, based on criminal history;
- require disclosure or authorization to release criminal history;
- demand a higher deposit or rent based on criminal history;
- refuse to add an immediate family member because of that person's criminal history;
- disqualify tenants from rental assistance programs such as Section 8, subject to exceptions;
- otherwise treat applicants or tenants differently based on criminal history.

A landlord who takes any action based on criminal history must give written notice and an opportunity to respond.

Providers must post the Notice to Rental Applicants and Tenants on application materials, on websites and at frequently visited locations.

The Cambridge Fair Housing Ordinance prohibits housing discrimination based on a protected category. Covered conduct includes viewing or renting an apartment, the terms and conditions of tenancy, and discriminatory advertising. Source of income, including Section 8 and public benefits, is a protected category, along with others such as race, disability, family status and military status.

Landlords, sellers and rental agents may use any reasonable standard to select tenants, as long as it is applied equally and uniformly to all applicants. Tenant selection may be based on the applicant's ability to pay the rent and ability to take care of the apartment and respect the rights of others.

After a hearing, the Cambridge Human Rights Commission may:
- levy civil fines of up to $300 for each violation;
- file a criminal complaint;
- seek injunctive relief;
- seek damages.

At or before the start of a tenancy, a lessor or the lessor's agent may not require a tenant or prospective tenant to pay more than:
- first month's rent;
- last month's rent at the same rate;
- a security deposit of no more than the first month's rent;
- the purchase and installation cost of a key and lock.

No other upfront charge is allowed, so landlord application or screening fees are not permitted. After the tenancy starts, the lessor may not demand advance rent beyond the current month or a deposit above the limit. No late-rent interest or penalty may be charged until 30 days after rent is due. A lessor must give a receipt for last-month rent paid in advance and pay interest on it.

A written notice to quit for nonpayment of rent to a residential tenant must come with a state form. The form must include documentation of any repayment agreements, plus information on:
- rental assistance programs, including RAFT;
- trial court rules and orders on summary process;
- federal and state eviction restrictions.

The form must prominently state that the notice to quit is not an eviction, that the tenant need not leave immediately, that the tenant is entitled to a legal proceeding, and that only a court order can force the tenant out. The executive office of housing and livable communities develops and posts the form, with the clause (ii) information in the 5 most common non-English languages. No court, including the Boston municipal court department, may accept a nonpayment summary process filing without proof the form was delivered.

